  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "William Boeing recognised aircraft-building talent in an unexpected place: the University of Washington’s rowing-shell workshop. Impressed by George and Dick Pocock’s craftsmanship, he recruited the brothers to make seaplane pontoons after securing a Navy training-aircraft order. Their team used western red cedar to produce floats that combined lightness with the strength needed for water landings.\n\nCommercial growth required reliable demand as well as skilled production. Encouraged by pilot Eddie Hubbard, Boeing entered the airmail business alongside manufacturing. Mail revenues helped finance passenger services and the development of airports and navigational aids. His airline operations also led to a school in Oakland where students could train for the expanding aviation industry.",
    "html": "<p>William Boeing recognised aircraft-building talent in an unexpected place: the University of Washington’s rowing-shell workshop. Impressed by George and Dick Pocock’s craftsmanship, he recruited the brothers to make seaplane pontoons after securing a Navy training-aircraft order. Their team used western red cedar to produce floats that combined lightness with the strength needed for water landings.</p>\r\n<p>Commercial growth required reliable demand as well as skilled production. Encouraged by pilot Eddie Hubbard, Boeing entered the airmail business alongside manufacturing. Mail revenues helped finance passenger services and the development of airports and navigational aids. His airline operations also led to a school in Oakland where students could train for the expanding aviation industry.</p>\r\n",
